  "answer_text": "**altavista.com** was the home of **AltaVista**, one of the most popular and historically significant early search engines in the history of the internet. \n\nIf you visit the URL today, it simply redirects to **Yahoo! Search**, as the service was officially shut down in 2013.\n\n### The Rise of AltaVista (1995\u20131997)\nLaunched on December 15, 1995, by researchers at Digital Equipment Corporation (DEC), AltaVista was a massive technological leap forward for the early web. Before AltaVista, most web directories (like Yahoo!) were curated manually. \n\nAltaVista revolutionized web search in several ways:\n* **The First Full-Text Index:** It was the first search engine that could crawl and index the full text of millions of web pages, making almost the entire early web searchable in real-time.\n* **Speed and Scale:** Powered by DEC\u2019s powerful 64-bit Alpha supercomputers and a fast, multi-threaded crawler named \"Scooter,\" AltaVista was incredibly fast. It processed millions of search queries a day at a time when the internet was still in its infancy.\n* **Babel Fish:** In 1997, AltaVista launched \"Babel Fish,\" the web's first free real-time translation service, which allowed users to translate web pages into several languages.\n* **The First CAPTCHA:** To prevent bots from spamming its submission index, AltaVista pioneered the early use of CAPTCHA technology.\n\n### The Decline\nDespite its technical superiority, AltaVista\u2019s parent company, DEC, didn't quite know what to do with it. After DEC was acquired by Compaq in 1998, they attempted to turn AltaVista into a \"web portal\" (similar to Yahoo!), cluttered with news, shopping, and weather, rather than keeping the focus on a clean, fast search experience.\n\nThis shift coincided with the rise of **Google**, which arrived with a clean, distraction-free homepage and a superior PageRank algorithm. Users quickly abandoned AltaVista for Google\u2019s faster and more relevant search results.\n\n### The End of an Era\n* **2003:** Overture Services purchased AltaVista, and later that same year, Yahoo! acquired Overture. Yahoo! essentially shelved the AltaVista brand, using Yahoo!'s own search technology behind the scenes.\n* **July 8, 2013:** After years of decline, Yahoo! officially shut down AltaVista. \n\nToday, AltaVista is remembered as the \"Google before Google\"\u2014the pioneering giant that proved just how powerful web search could be.",
